# Overview

# Storage

Here we cover the intended usage, and implementation details of the compiled data storage format. For more information
on the data stored in the files and data formatting itself, check out the
[Compiled Data Format](https://github.com/Weill-Neurohub-OPTiMaL/ProjectDocs/wiki/Compiled-Data-Format) documentation.

## Usage

### Reading Data

To read the data stored in an HDF5 file, simply use the relevant data reader. All of these can be imported from 
`server.storage`. For example, if you would like to read all the accelerometer data:

```python
from server.storage import NeuralReader
reader = NeuralReader(r'path/to/the/file.h5')
neural = reader.read()
```
This will give you a pandas DataFrame in `accel`. The index will be a pandas DatetimeIndex, and the columns will be a
MultiIndex. The specific values of the MultiIndex will depend on the modality you are using (see below). For this,
assume that the returned Dataframe has the following format:

|          | Left/M1 | Left/STN | Right/M1 | Right/STN |
|----------|---------|----------|----------|-----------|
| DateTime |   ###   |   ###    |    ###   |    ###    |   

You can access any individual column (in this case the Left STN) using:
```python
column = neural[('Left', 'STN')]
```
Or you can access multiple columns using (in this case all the M1 data)
```python
all_m1_data = neural.xs('M1', axis=1)
```

If you are using the `CombinedReader` the you will be able to easily access data from multiple modalities. Initialize
the reader with :
```python
from server.storage import CombinedReader
reader = CombinedReader(r'path/to/the/file.h5')
```
You can then access and read each modality with the attributes of the reader like this:
```python
accel = reader.accel.read()
stim = reader.settings.stim.read()
```
Each call to `read()` will return a `pd.DataFrame` as with the individual modality readers.

### Gather New Data

To gather new data and write it into the combined data format takes a bit of preparation. You will need
1. Wasabi access via RClone, with the remote available at `secret_sauce:/`
2. Rune API access to download the apple watch data
3. (Upcoming) VPN access to download KeyLogger and PoseData
4. Ensure that the following environment variables are set:
   - `OUTPUT_BASE`: Specify the directory to output the ready HDF5 files
   - `RUNE_AUTH_LOC`: Path to the .yaml file which contains your RUNE API access tokens
   - `LOCAL_RAW`: directory where you want the local cache of the RC+S data to be stored.

Although not necessary, it's recommended to generate all the time alignments ahead of time, as this allows you to 
parallel process the data later and greatly speeds up the whole process. You can do this with the `rebuild_all_align.py`
script. See the [doc-string](https://github.com/Weill-Neurohub-OPTiMaL/data-net-server/tree/main/scripts/build_all_alignments.py) 
for more information.

To collect data into the compiled format use the `fetch_patient` function in `server.storage.ingest`.

